    "id": "natural-testosterone-and-protein-enhancement",
    "roleId": "research-papers",
    "title": "Natural Testosterone and Protein Enhancement",
    "status": "Active",
    "date": "2026-06",
    "summary": "The project titled 'Natural Testosterone and Protein Enhancement' is a comprehensive meta-analysis examining the efficacy and side-effect profiles of natural testosterone-enhancing compounds and protein supplements within the global sports nutrition market. It synthesises findings from 74 peer-reviewed studies, providing insights into the physiological benefits and adverse outcomes associated with these supplements.",
    "objective": "The project aimed to evaluate the effectiveness and safety of natural testosterone boosters and protein supplements, addressing the fragmented evidence base in this rapidly growing market. It sought to map dose-response relationships for benefits such as lean mass gain and strength improvement against potential harms like gastrointestinal disturbances.",
    "context": "The global sports nutrition market surpassed $45 billion in 2025, with testosterone boosters and protein supplements being among its fastest-growing segments. The increasing consumer demand for muscle growth support, coupled with the well-documented side effects of synthetic anabolic agents, has heightened interest in natural alternatives that claim to offer similar benefits with fewer adverse outcomes.",
    "approach": "The analysis utilised a meta-analytic framework to synthesise data from 74 studies published between 2004 and 2025, employing statistical measures such as Cohen's d to evaluate effect sizes and adverse event rates. It also examined dose-response relationships to assess the impact of various compounds on physiological outcomes.",
    "result": "The findings revealed significant efficacy for compounds like Fenugreek Extract and Ashwagandha (KSM-66), with p-values of <0.001 and 0.001 respectively, indicating strong statistical significance. The study also highlighted adverse event rates, with Fenugreek showing a 9.2% incidence of gastrointestinal discomfort compared to a 5.3% rate in the placebo group.",
